my express server crashes completely when a db query fails inside an async route, instead of going to my error handler. why

app.get('/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await db.getUser(req.params.id)
  res.json(user)
})

You're on Express 4, which doesn't handle promises: when the async function rejects, Express never sees the error, so it becomes an unhandled promise rejection, and since Node 15 an unhandled rejection terminates the process by default.

You have three options.

**Upgrade to Express 5.** It forwards a rejected promise from a route or middleware to `next(err)` automatically, so your code works as written and reaches the error handler. Read the migration guide first: some things changed, for example the wildcard route syntax (`*` must now be named, like `/*splat`) and `req.body` is `undefined` when no body parser ran.

**Stay on 4 and catch explicitly:**

```js
app.get("/users/:id", async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const user = await db.getUser(req.params.id);
    res.json(user);
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});
```

**Stay on 4 with a small wrapper,** so you don't repeat the try/catch:

```js
const asyncHandler = (fn) => (req, res, next) =>
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);

app.get("/users/:id", asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const user = await db.getUser(req.params.id);
  res.json(user);
}));
```

Whichever you pick, you also need an error handler with four parameters registered after all routes:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);
  res.status(500).json({ error: "Internal server error" });
});
```

Don't send `err.message` to the client for unexpected errors: it can leak SQL or internal details.

